      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kXWbuE3c8H1DP_Mx9dg5d",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 346.86832392410696,
      "y": 1564.7499995714284,
      "width": 742.1396484375,
      "height": 750,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#a5d8ff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b2p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 105374074,
      "version": 2335,
      "versionNonce": 1319347366,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1795104418425,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Each phase adds 2 terms to every thread's total,\nbecause a piece is 2 wide. 4 terms in all, so 2 phases.\n\nThe terms are the same as in the plain row dot column.\nOnly where the values are read from has changed.\n\nThe block touches 16 different values:\n2 rows of A (8 values) and 2 columns of B (8 values).\nEach one is needed by two threads. Example: The 1 in A1 is\nused by T0 and T1.\n\nWithout tiling, every thread fetches its own row and column from global:\n4 threads x 8 values = 32 global reads. Each value is fetched twice.\n\nWith tiling, each value is fetched from global once and the\n2 threads share the copy: 16 global reads.\n\nRule of thumb: the wider the piece, the more threads share each\nfetched value. Global reads drop by the piece width.\n\nTiling does not reduce how many values a thread reads: each thread\nstill does 8 reads. It changes where they come from. The tile is put in\nshared memory once, and the threads share those values instead of \neach fetching their own from global.\n\nThe only global reads left are the ones that fill the tiles: one per\nvalue.\n\nRule of thumb: the same number of reads, but most of them move from slow\nglobal memory to fast shared memory.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Each phase adds 2 terms to every thread's total,\nbecause a piece is 2 wide. 4 terms in all, so 2 phases.\n\nThe terms are the same as in the plain row dot column.\nOnly where the values are read from has changed.\n\nThe block touches 16 different values:\n2 rows of A (8 values) and 2 columns of B (8 values).\nEach one is needed by two threads. Example: The 1 in A1 is\nused by T0 and T1.\n\nWithout tiling, every thread fetches its own row and column from global:\n4 threads x 8 values = 32 global reads. Each value is fetched twice.\n\nWith tiling, each value is fetched from global once and the\n2 threads share the copy: 16 global reads.\n\nRule of thumb: the wider the piece, the more threads share each\nfetched value. Global reads drop by the piece width.\n\nTiling does not reduce how many values a thread reads: each thread\nstill does 8 reads. It changes where they come from. The tile is put in\nshared memory once, and the threads share those values instead of \neach fetching their own from global.\n\nThe only global reads left are the ones that fill the tiles: one per\nvalue.\n\nRule of thumb: the same number of reads, but most of them move from slow\nglobal memory to fast shared memory.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
